{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"legal positivism legal positivism","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md","fileName":"Legal Positivism.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Legal Positivism","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-17 09:48\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Privacy Theory and Legal Limits]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Privacy Theory and Legal Limits","Intrinsic Limit of Law","frontiersofdatascience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"legal positivism legal positivism","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md","fileName":"Legal Positivism.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Legal Positivism","heading":"Legal Positivism","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Legal positivism explains law through social rules and institutional practices rather than making legal validity depend directly on moral merit. A legal order identifies which norms count as law through criteria accepted and used by its officials.\n\nThe framework permits a distinction between what the law is and whether it is morally justified. That separation makes it possible to ask whether privacy is merely enacted protection or an [[Intrinsic Limit of Law]] needed for a viable legal order.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Privacy Theory and Legal Limits","Intrinsic Limit of Law","frontiersofdatascience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Legal Positivism.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"legal positivism legal posit